    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Have you had this restless, easily distracted way of being since you were a child?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, goodness, yes. I suppose I have. Even back in grade school, my teachers used to tell my mother that I was bright but just so scattered. I was always losing my school papers, talking out of turn, and wiggling around in my seat before I’d even finished my work. \n\nMy third-grade teacher, Mrs. Gable, she used to get so frustrated with me. She was a very stern woman, always wore these heavy wool skirts, even in the spring. I don't know how she didn't just melt! I can't stand wool against my skin, it makes me itch terribly. I have to buy all my sweaters in cotton or those nice bamboo blends they make now. Have you felt those? They're incredibly soft. My daughter bought me a beautiful pale blue one for Christmas last year, wrapped it in this gorgeous silver paper with a big velvet bow. I always save the bows from presents, you know, because you can just stick a bit of tape on the back and use them again next year. It seems so wasteful to throw them away, especially the nice fabric ones. My husband used to tease me about my big box of recycled bows in the closet... \n\nWait, what were we talking about? Oh, childhood! Yes, I was quite the wiggly little girl, always losing things and talking too much.",
